[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and closed on 5 October: engineering fixed the
parser and retired the booking-v3 form. Read report by report, the ticket holds three problems,
and the fixes cover only two of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Dates that move by <b>one day</b> are still open. Neither fix touches them, and they had no ticket.
</Callout>

## The three problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} filed={filed} />

- **parser.** The shared parser read every date month first, and every tenant here writes the day
  first, so 7 April came out as 4 July. Fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- **hint.** booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field but reads the date day
  first. People type what the hint says. Only the `form` channel of booking-v3 tenants. Won't fix:
  the form is retired.
- **shift.** The date moves by a day: 12 May shows as 11 May, Tuesday as Monday, one meeting on
  different days in Lisbon and Toronto. That is a time-zone shift, not a flipped month. The desk
  already noted these (`desk-notes.json`), but `causeOf` files every report by tenant, so they
  were counted under the parser and closed with it.

## Why one ticket hid three

- The reports came in under a dozen different subject lines, so [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
  would have kept them apart. They were merged by topic instead, "dates", most of them in one
  sitting on 2 July. Nobody grouped them by cause.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with the tenant's form, so
  the tags in the table don't add up to the problems.
- The export repeats some reports across pages. Counts here are by report id, once each.

## For the desk

- Merge by cause, not by topic: a report joins a ticket when `problemOf` puts it there.
- A report that reads as a one-day move goes to the shift ticket, not #4471.
- [Triage: duplicate reports](/kb/triage/dedupe) was last reviewed in June 2024 and says "when
  unsure, merge". Re-read it before the next big ticket.

